$il$# Clean sheet: what a go-to-market system for Pier should look like if nothing had to be replicated

**v01, 15 September 2026, Oliver Müller (written with Claude Code), C2 Commercial in Confidence**
**For: Brad Gordon and his Claude. Read after `260915_PIER_lovable_single_gtm_system_crm_analysis_v02_OM_C2.md`. Where the two disagree, this file is the design intent and the v02 file is the inventory.**

---

## 0. The question this answers

The v02 analysis asked "what would it take to move what monday does into Supabase and Lovable". This file asks the harder question Oliver put afterwards: **if we had a clean sheet, with Supabase already holding the top of the funnel and monday holding the deals, what is the best system, and how much of what we built in monday deserves to exist at all?**

The method is simple. Every field, board, automation and habit in monday was tested against one question: **is this a fact somebody learned, or a workaround for something monday could not compute?** Facts survive. Workarounds die. What is left is small.

---

## 1. Verdict in one page

**Roughly two thirds of what monday holds should not be rebuilt.** Not because it was wrong, but because most of it exists to make a spreadsheet-shaped tool behave like a database.

| What monday has | Count | Verdict | Why |
|---|---|---|---|
| Typed status vocabularies (Deals Stage, Companies Sales Status, Companies Status, Contacts Type, Contacts Priority, Pipeline health, Health, Stage status, Basis, Activity flag) | 10 | **Keep 1** (Stage) | Everything else is either derivable from stage, touches and tasks, or a duplicate of another field. Companies carries three overlapping status columns today |
| Deal columns typed or stamped by hand or automation | about 25 | **Keep 8** | company, owner, stage, expected close, park-until, three value overrides. Next Step Date, Last interaction, Stage changed, Days in stage, Health, Stage status, Basis, Weighted, Year-3, Business case tick, Country, Deal Source (derivable), the mirrors and the flag all go |
| Activities board rows | 676 | **Keep about 60** | 450 or so are "I sent a message" records that Supabase already holds as touches. Only calls, meetings and internal to-dos are tasks. The board is 70% duplication of the message log |
| Activity type labels (live and dead) | 18 | **Keep 0** | A touch is a channel plus a position in a sequence, two fields that already exist. A chaser is not a type of thing |
| Automations on Deals and Activities | 17 | **Keep 1** (un-park) | Fourteen exist to stamp dates, move groups or copy flags so that a formula somewhere can read them. In a database those are queries |
| Dashboard widgets | about 16 | **Keep 6 numbers and 3 charts** | Half the widgets exist because monday cannot filter a widget by a computed value, so a column was created to hold the value. The weekly report is what Mark asked for; the dashboard was our idea |
| Emails & Activities timeline | one per item | **Keep, and make it the centre** | The one thing monday does that is a fact record, not a workaround. Rebuild it as a single interactions table across every channel and every object |
| monday Notetaker transcripts | per meeting | **Keep the transcript, not the tool** | Transcripts come from Teams through Graph; the summary comes from our own draft stack, which knows Pier's rules |
| Outlook BCC integration | one address | **Kill** | Lossy, breaks for Ticketplan items, needs a human to remember the BCC. Capture from the mailbox instead |
| AddToCRM | one subscription | **Kill** | The Supabase watchers already do it |
| Two logins for one person | | **Kill** | One account, one person, roles on it |

**The design that falls out has five nouns and one verb.** Company, Person, Interaction, Deal, Play. The verb is "next", meaning the system always knows the next action for every record and Oliver only ever sees a list of them. Section 3 defines the nouns; section 4 the engine; section 5 the channels; section 6 the human's day; section 7 what to stop doing in Claude Code as well.

---

## 2. The principle, stated once

**A fact is recorded once, at the moment it happens, by the channel it happens in. Every status, health, priority, value and next step is computed from facts. Nothing is typed that could be derived, and nothing is derived twice.**

Test any proposed field against it. monday fails the test about 40 times. The current Supabase passes it nearly everywhere in the cold funnel (the chase state machine, the refusal gate, the send effects function) and this file simply extends the same discipline over the deal.

Corollaries, because each has cost us before:

1. **A typed date rots.** Next Step Date was typed and read "overdue" on Revendo while the thread was live that morning. Dates come from tasks and touches, never from a cell.
2. **A status vocabulary is a query with a name.** "Engaged", "Open Deal", "Active Lead" and "In Monday" are four spellings of "has a live conversation". One query, no spellings.
3. **A workaround becomes a field, and the field becomes a habit.** Pipeline health had to be refreshed by hand before each meeting because monday could not compute it. Nobody questioned it for a month.
4. **Approval is the one human step in a send, and it should be the only one.** Today a chaser is typed into a Draft column, pasted into Outlook, sent, marked Done, given an Outcome, and the Next Step Date is re-derived. Six actions for one email. The target is one: approve.

---

## 3. The object model: five nouns

### 3.1 Company

Exactly what `companies` is today, minus the fields that duplicate a deal or a derived state. Keep: identity, country (as a key, not free text), parent group, product line, research stage, insurance state as data, size history with rungs, owner, score. Drop: `opportunity_status` as a typed field, `tracking` for anything except the EUREFAS flags, `monday_deal_id`, `account_owner`. The company's **engine mode** (cold, in conversation, in deal, partner, lost, parked) is a view over deals and interactions, never a column.

### 3.2 Person

Exactly what `contacts` is today. It already carries the chase state machine, the promise of quiet, DNC, cooldown and provenance. Two additions only: `deal_contacts` roles when a deal exists, and the monday Language dropdown carried into `formality` where blank. Drop from the monday side: Type, Priority, Seniority as free text.

### 3.3 Interaction

**This is the centre of the clean sheet and the one table monday never had.** One row per touch in any channel, in either direction, on any object: a connection request, a LinkedIn message, an InMail, an email, a call, a meeting, an event conversation, a note. `outreach_log` is already 85% of this; it needs `deal_id`, `direction`, `email_thread_id`, `meeting_id` and touch types for call, meeting and note. The timeline of a company, a person or a deal is then one query with three joins, and "last interaction" is `max(occurred_at)`, never a stamp.

Two consequences. The Activities board disappears as a concept, because a sent message is an interaction, not a completed task. And the monday habit of logging "Sales Navigator" as an activity after sending disappears, because the watcher already recorded the send.

### 3.4 Deal

A company in a commercial conversation, with eight typed fields: company, owner (plus optional co-owner), stage, expected close date, park-until date, and three value overrides (attachment, GWP per policy, Pier share) with a note. `deal_stage_history` gives every date the old board stamped. Value, weighting, basis, days in stage, moving or stuck, next step and last interaction are all views (v02 section 5).

**Recommend collapsing the stage ladder from seven to five.** Opportunity and Discovery are weighted 5% and 10% with no calibration and no won deals; the boundary between them has never been defined in writing; and 17 of 28 active deals sit in Discovery today, which says the distinction is not doing work. Proposed ladder: **Conversation, Proposal, Negotiation, Won, Lost**, with **Parked** as a state on any open stage (a deal parked at Proposal is still a Proposal) carrying the revisit date. Weights: Conversation 10, Proposal 30, Negotiation 60. This is Mark's to rule on, because the weights are his; the recommendation is recorded here so it is asked, not assumed.

**Drop from the deal:** Year-3 run-rate (context nobody reads; a note in the value method is enough), the Business case checkbox (derive from a document of type business_case existing on the deal), Country (the company's), Deal Source as a typed status (derive from the first interaction's channel; keep a typed override only for Referral and Event, which cannot be inferred), Current IPID (belongs on the company's insurance state).

### 3.5 Play

The generalisation that makes cold outreach and deal follow-up one system instead of two. A play is a rule set: **trigger, steps, exit conditions, approval mode, caps**. The current chase engine is one hardcoded play (CR sent → accepted → first message → 7 days → chaser, capped per channel). Brad's campaigns idea is the same thing with a manual enrolment trigger. Deal follow-up is the same thing with a different voice layer and a different approval rule. Nurture after a lost deal or a withdrawn CR is the same thing on a monthly rhythm with content instead of asks.

```
plays
  id, team_id, product_line, name, purpose enum (cold_sequence | deal_followup | nurture | event_followup | reengagement),
  trigger enum (cr_accepted | positive_reply | deal_opened | deal_lost | manual_enrol | event_attended | signal_detected),
  approval enum (approve_each | approve_batch | auto_within_caps),
  active bool, created_by, version

play_steps
  play_id, step_no, delay_days, channel, message_type (maps to voice_assets.applies_to),
  cap_per_contact, stop_on enum[] (reply | dnc | deal_open | promise_of_quiet | cooldown | unsubscribed)

play_enrolments
  play_id, contact_id, deal_id nullable, enrolled_at, current_step, next_due_on, state (active | paused | exited), exit_reason
```

The chase engine becomes the runner of every enrolment whose `next_due_on` is today, calling the same gate (`fn_evaluate_gates`) and the same drafter, and producing rows in the same draft queue. One engine, one queue, one approval screen, for a cold InMail chaser and for a warm email to Revendo alike. **This is the single largest efficiency gain available**, because today the cold side runs in Supabase and the warm side runs in a person's head plus a monday column.

### 3.6 The two small nouns

**Task**: a non-message action with a due date and an owner (call, meeting to hold, send the proposal, internal). About 60 of monday's 676 activities. **Note**: free text pinned to any object, the monday Updates tab. Both feed the timeline. Neither holds a draft.

---

## 4. The engine: how "next" is decided

Every record has one computed `next_step` and one `next_due_on`. Today reads them, ordered by value at stake. This replaces Health, Stage status, Pipeline health, Next Step Date, the P0 to P3 priority scale and the afternoon board at the same time.

| Record | Derived from | Reads as |
|---|---|---|
| Company, cold | research stage, score, contact count, gates | research now / source leads / outreach ready / walk the checkout / nothing |
| Person, in a play | enrolment step, gate result, reply state | approve draft / answer reply / waiting until date / blocked: reason |
| Deal | open tasks, last interaction, stage age, park date | task due: call Matthias / no next step planned / stale 21 days / stuck 61 days in Conversation / revisit due |
| Play | enrolments due, caps remaining | 7 drafts to approve, 12 sends scheduled |

Health as seven labels was a monday workaround for "I cannot sort a board by a computed value". In Lovable the sort is the health. Keep the labels only as filter chips on the deal board.

---

## 5. Channels: what integrates, how deep, and what stays human

| Channel | Capture (in) | Send (out) | Human step |
|---|---|---|---|
| LinkedIn and Sales Navigator | Already done: three watchers, alias matching, reply classification, idempotent capture | **Stays manual** (LinkedIn terms; account safety). One click opens the thread with the approved draft on the clipboard; the watcher records the send | Click, paste, send |
| InMail | As above; credit ledger exists | Manual, same one click; credit deducted by the ledger at send | Approve, click, paste |
| Email (Outlook) | **New: Graph mailbox capture**, both directions, matched to persons and deals by address and thread; unmatched queue; calendar noise filtered | **New: system send after approval** via Graph on the rep's behalf, at a scheduled time, only where `markets.cold_email_legal` or the thread is warm. This is where the six-actions-per-chaser problem goes to one | Approve (batch, each morning) |
| Phone and Teams | Teams transcript via Graph after the meeting; call logged as an interaction with one tap | n/a | Hold the call; accept the drafted summary and tasks |
| Events | Event list pasted as intake; attendees matched or created; enrolled in the event follow-up play | Through the plays | Approve the batch |
| LinkedIn content (Oliver's posts) | Post published → interaction of type content on every connection who engaged | Manual | Write the post |
| Web signals | Research agent on Supabase (blocked on the Paul permission) writes `signals` with decay | n/a | None |

**What deliberately stays human, and why.** Sourcing people on LinkedIn (judgement about who decides; title matching fails at scale, the Chibo lesson). Sending on LinkedIn (terms of service). Approving every send (Oliver's rule, and the only quality gate that has never failed). Holding the conversation. Everything around those four is the system's job.

---

## 6. Oliver's day on the clean sheet

1. Open Today. Six counts: replies to answer, drafts to approve, deal tasks due, deals needing a decision (stuck, slipped, revisit due), companies to source, blocked overnight.
2. Answer replies. Each reply card shows the classification, the thread, and a drafted answer already queued; approve or edit.
3. Approve drafts in one pass: cold chasers, first messages, warm deal follow-ups, nurture touches, all in one queue, each card showing who, why now, the wedge, the guard result and the play it belongs to. Email sends itself at 09:30. LinkedIn drafts wait on the clipboard for the click.
4. Work the deal tasks: the call, the proposal, the meeting. The summary and next tasks are drafted from the transcript.
5. Paste anything new into the intake: an event list, a name Mark mentioned. It is triaged overnight.
6. Friday: the report goes to Mark without anyone building it.

Six steps, one system, one login. Against today's day: the afternoon board in Claude Code, the Lovable queue, monday's Open - Today view, Outlook, Sales Navigator and the LinkedIn inbox, with the chaser skill bridging two of them.

---

## 7. What to stop doing, including in Claude Code

The clean sheet is not only about monday. Several things in the console exist because two systems had to be kept in step, and they go when there is one.

| Retire | Because |
|---|---|
| `create-monday-chaser` skill, `monday_guard.py`, the 2,000-character rule | No monday column to fill |
| `outreach_gate.MONDAY_ACTIVE`, `is_monday_company`, the `In Monday` tracking flag, `BLOCKED - worked in Monday` next actions | Engine mode is derived from deals |
| The afternoon board artifact and its LOG-BACK loop | Today in Lovable is the board, and sends record themselves |
| `predraft.py`, `verify_batch.py`, `check_cards` as separate Python gates | `fn_evaluate_gates` already is the gate; the lint rules that still live only in Python (F61 no-repetition, the message-card format) move into the drafter |
| The xlsx lake and `lake_common.py` guardrails | Frozen since 3 September; Supabase is the record |
| Cowork briefs for lead sourcing as documents | Sourcing queue in Lovable is the brief; the watcher is the return path |
| Living Context entries as the way rules reach the drafter | `voice_assets` and `pier_ea_documents` are versioned in the database; Claude Code pushes a new version, nothing is re-typed |

Claude Code's remaining job becomes what it is good at: writing and versioning the rules and voice layers, running the research agent, doing the occasional analysis like this one, and being the place Oliver argues with the system. Daily operation lives in Lovable.

