---

## 10. What this file does not claim

It does not size Brad's effort; the v02 file does that per block, and the plays engine adds roughly one block of similar size. It does not settle the stage weights or the 2,000 device floor, both of which are Mark's. It assumes Graph consent is obtainable on the Pier tenant. And it keeps every guard from the master handover exactly as it is: the clean sheet is about removing workarounds, never about removing the things that refuse.

---

## 11. The blueprint on one page

This is the same design as sections 3 to 6, condensed to the page Brad can put next to his screen.

**One database, one front end, one login per person. Five record types and nothing else.**

| Record | What it is | Typed by a human | Everything else |
|---|---|---|---|
| Company | the account: identity, country, group, product line, research state, insurance state, size history, score | name, source, product line, owner | mode (cold, in conversation, in deal, partner, lost, parked), status, priority: all computed |
| Person | a decision maker at a company | LinkedIn URL when sourced; roles on a deal | connection state, chase state, cooldown, DNC, formality: captured or derived |
| Interaction | every touch in every channel, both directions, on any record: CR, LinkedIn message, InMail, email, call, meeting, event conversation, note | nothing; the channels write it | the timeline, last interaction, reply state |
| Deal | a company in a commercial conversation | company, owner, stage (Conversation, Proposal, Negotiation, Won, Lost), expected close, park-until, three value overrides | value, weighting, basis, days in stage, moving or stuck, next step, health |
| Play | a rule set: trigger, steps, exit conditions, approval mode, caps | the play definition, once | enrolments, due dates, drafts |

Plus Task (call, meeting, internal to-do) and Note.

**The engine.** Every record carries one computed next step and one due date. Today is the list of those, ordered by value at stake. Plays are the one automation: cold sequence, deal follow-up, nurture, event follow-up and re-engagement are the same runner with different triggers, voice layers and approval rules. Every draft passes the existing refusal gate and lands in one approval queue.

**The channels.**

| Channel | In | Out |
|---|---|---|
| LinkedIn and Sales Navigator | the three watchers, already live | manual by LinkedIn's terms; one click opens the thread with the approved draft on the clipboard, the watcher records the send |
| Email | Graph mailbox capture, both directions, matched by address and thread, calendar noise filtered, no BCC | sent by the system after approval, at a scheduled time, only in legal markets or warm threads |
| Phone and Teams | transcript via Graph after the meeting; a call logged with one tap | summary and follow-up tasks drafted from the transcript |
| Events | attendee list pasted in, matched, enrolled in the event play | through the play |
| Oliver's LinkedIn posts | engagement captured as interactions on connections | manual |
| Web signals | research agent writes decaying signals that lift a company's next step | none |

**The day.** Open Today, six counts. Answer replies, each with a drafted answer waiting. Approve drafts in one pass; email sends itself at 09:30, LinkedIn waits for the click. Work the deal tasks. Paste anything new into the intake. On Friday the report reaches Mark without anyone building it.

**What stays human, deliberately.** Choosing who to source, sending on LinkedIn, approving every send, holding the conversation.

**What it removes.** monday for Pier Protect, AddToCRM, the Notetaker once meetings have a home, the Outlook BCC address, the second login, and the console plumbing that kept two systems aligned.

---

## 12. Two ways to get there, and a suggested path

The v02 analysis describes **Path A: replicate**. Build the deal layer as monday has it, migrate, then simplify. Its strength is that nothing is lost and every acceptance test maps to something visible today. Its weakness is that it carries the workarounds across before removing them, so the team builds Health labels and stage-status logic it will delete a month later.

This file describes **Path B: clean sheet**. Build the five nouns and the plays engine, migrate only facts (deals, interactions, tasks, transcripts), and never build the workarounds. Its strength is a smaller system. Its weakness is that it changes the stage ladder and the approval habits at the same time as the tooling, which is two changes at once for the people using it.

**Suggested path: B for the schema, A for the sequencing.** Build the clean data model from day one (it is smaller than the replicated one, so it is not more work), but bring the reps across in the v02 order: deals first, then tasks and timeline, then email, then reporting, then decommission. Concretely:

1. **Week 1.** Interaction table extended (`deal_id`, `direction`, `email_thread_id`, call and meeting types), `deals` with five stages and a `parked_until` state, `deal_stage_history`, `deal_contacts`, `tasks`, `notes`, `meetings`, `valuation_standards`, `pipeline_stage_config`, and the views for value and next step. Migrate the 33 monday deals, 60 or so real tasks, and the deal timelines as interactions. Seven monday stages fold into five at import; the mapping is recorded on each stage-history row.
2. **Week 2.** The Deals board and deal page in Lovable. Today gains the deal buckets. The company engine mode goes live and the cold engine respects it. Oliver and Jack work deals in Lovable; monday goes read-only.
3. **Weeks 2 to 4, in parallel.** Graph mail capture in shadow next to the BCC; the plays runner built as a generalisation of the chase engine, with the current cold sequence expressed as play number one and a deal follow-up play as number two. Meetings intake from transcripts.
4. **Week 5.** System-sent email after batch approval switched on for warm threads and legal markets, once the two-week shadow capture is clean. The weekly report goes to Mark from Lovable. Nurture and event plays added.
5. **Week 6.** BCC off, AddToCRM cancelled, Pier Protect workspace in monday archived, console plumbing in section 7 retired one item at a time.

The acceptance tests in the v02 file still apply; add three: a cold chaser and a warm deal follow-up both appear in the same approval queue on the same morning; an approved email to a French contact sends itself at the scheduled time and appears as an interaction on the deal within a minute; a company whose deal is lost enters the nurture play automatically and leaves it the moment a reply arrives.

---

## 13. Brad: spar against this before building any of it

Oliver's instruction is that this design is to be argued with, not mirrored. The 9 September start-here note said the same about the master handover and it produced better decisions than the original. So, before writing a migration:

**Read it looking for the places it is wrong.** Likely candidates, in Oliver's own words where they exist:

1. **Five stages.** Is collapsing Opportunity and Discovery a simplification or a loss of signal? Jack works fewer, larger UK deals; does he need the distinction that Oliver's DACH funnel does not? Ask him.
2. **Plays as the one engine.** Is a warm deal follow-up really the same machine as a cold InMail chaser, or does forcing them together produce a runner with so many exceptions that two simple engines would be cleaner? The test is the exit conditions: if the list of stop reasons is the same for both, one engine is right.
3. **System-sent email.** Does sending on the rep's behalf through Graph create a deliverability, compliance or trust problem that pasting into Outlook did not? Phil should be asked once about the compliance comfort. Is a 09:30 batch better than sending at approval time?
4. **The Interaction table as a single spine.** Is one table for CRs, emails, meetings and notes a clean design or a wide table with many nullable columns? Would `interactions` plus typed satellite tables (`emails`, `meetings`) be easier to query and index?
5. **Derived company mode.** A view that decides whether the cold engine may touch a company is on the critical path of every send. Is a view fast and safe enough, or should the mode be a materialised column maintained by the same triggers that write deals and interactions, with the view as the check?
6. **The retirement list in section 7.** Which of the console guards encode a rule that is NOT yet in `fn_evaluate_gates` or the drafter? The F61 no-repetition lint is one known case. List them before anything is switched off.
7. **What is missing.** Partner management after Won (onboarding, programme reviews, renewals) is out of scope here on purpose. Is that the right cut, or does a Won deal need a home on day one so it does not fall back into a spreadsheet?

**Then report back in this shape**, so Oliver can rule quickly: for each point, agree or disagree, the reason in two or three sentences, and what you would build instead. Where you disagree and the evidence is in the database, run the query and quote the number; that is how the cooldown defect and the group collisions were found. Oliver will read it, rule on the contested points, and the ruling goes into this file as a dated note before the first migration runs.

**Two things are not up for sparring**, because they are the lessons this system has already paid for: every guard fails closed, and Oliver approves every send.
